OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20161222
LR  - 20181113
IS  - 1433-0385 (Electronic)
IS  - 0009-4722 (Linking)
VI  - 87
IP  - 1
DP  - 2016 Jan
TI  - [Tracheostomy techniques].
PG  - 73-83; quiz 84-5
LID - 10.1007/s00104-015-0116-7 [doi]
AB  - Due to the comprehensive establishment of modern techniques, tracheostomy has 
      become a routine procedure in intensive care units (ICU). The negative effects of 
      prolonged translaryngeal intubation on the laryngeal and tracheal mucosa up to 
      tracheal stenosis can be reduced by tracheostomy. Furthermore, long-term 
      ventilation is facilitated; however, there is no clear evidence on the optimal 
      timing of tracheostomy in critically ill patients. The specific indications and 
      contraindications of surgical as well as percutaneous tracheostomy must be 
      strictly observed for a safe and successful intervention. Exchanging the 
      tracheostomy tube may lead to potentially dangerous situations especially after 
      percutaneous tracheostomy. A standardized and structured approach is therefore 
      recommended.
