TI  - A Safe Approach to Percutaneous Tracheostomy for COVID-19 Patients in Intensive 
      Care.
PG  - e14663
LID - 10.7759/cureus.14663 [doi]
LID - e14663
AB  - The novel coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) has placed a burden on critical 
      care facilities worldwide. Patients who remain critically unwell with COVID-19 
      require prolonged periods of ventilation, and the burden of both the resources 
      during a pandemic and the slow respiratory wean must be managed. Percutaneous 
      tracheostomies are commonplace in long-term intensive care patients, yet little 
      is known about their role in COVID-19, particularly how operator safety is 
      maintained during the procedure. Here, we describe an approach designed to 
      minimize cross-infection of the operators undertaking percutaneous tracheostomies 
      within this subset of patients. Focus should be on non-technical skills, 
      prolonged periods of pre-oxygenation, and minimal ventilation during the 
      procedure to minimize the risk of aerosolization generated from an open breathing 
      system. Our modified technique demonstrates successful early experiences with no 
      operators testing positive for COVID-19 or developing symptoms following any 
      performed procedure.
