PMID- 21991172
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
DCOM- 20111110
LR  - 20240324
IS  - 1592-9558 (Print)
IS  - 1592-9566 (Electronic)
IS  - 1592-9558 (Linking)
VI  - 22
IP  - 3
DP  - 2009 Sep 30
TI  - Emergency percutaneous tracheostomy in a severely burned patient with upper 
      airway obstruction and circulatory failure.
PG  - 152-4
AB  - Airway inaccessibility is one of the most dreaded situations in emergency 
      medicine. Surgical tracheostomy is not indicated in emergency situations because 
      it takes a long time and can result in death if respiratory support cannot be 
      provided during the procedure. Emergency percutaneous tracheostomy (PCT) was 
      widely regarded as absolutely counterindicated. Recently, however, a number of 
      studies have appeared on the safety and feasibility of PCT in situations regarded 
      as presenting relative contraindications. We describe the life-saving action of 
      Griggs' PCT in a patient with upper airway obstruction resulting from burns, 
      smoke injuries, and unsuccessful tracheal intubation attempts. Emergency PCT 
      using the Griggs technique was immediately performed without aseptic care, and a 
      9-mm internal diameter tracheostomy tube was successfully inserted in less than 
      one minute. Griggs' PCT is a quick technique that secures an airway when tracheal 
      intubation fails. The feasibility - in selected cases - of using an emergency 
      Griggs' PCT, in experienced hands, rather than cricothyroidotomy or surgical 
      tracheostomy, is recommended.
