TI  - Venous air embolus during percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy: A case report.
PG  - 354-356
LID - 10.1177/1751143718755015 [doi]
AB  - We describe the first case of venous air embolism during percutaneous 
      tracheostomy performed during continuous renal replacement therapy with citrate 
      anticoagulation. Venous air embolism is a known risk during percutaneous 
      dilatational tracheostomy; concurrent use of extracorporeal circuits may present 
      a compounded risk due to the negative pressure developed by the blood pump. Use 
      of regional citrate anticoagulation, permitting invasive procedures to be 
      performed without discontinuing renal replacement therapy, may lead to an 
      increase in the incidence of venous air embolism during tracheostomy.
