PMID- 18289238
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20080318
LR  - 20081223
IS  - 1365-2044 (Electronic)
IS  - 0003-2409 (Linking)
VI  - 63
IP  - 3
DP  - 2008 Mar
TI  - An investigation into the length of standard tracheostomy tubes in critical care 
      patients.
PG  - 302-6
LID - 10.1111/j.1365-2044.2007.05327.x [doi]
AB  - A number of problems have been reported with the use of standard length 
      tracheostomy tubes in adult critical care patients. We measured the depth from 
      the skin surface to the tracheal wall and the angle of the tracheal stoma during 
      percutaneous tracheostomy. In vitro measurements were also performed on standard 
      tracheostomy tubes. Comparison of in vivo and in vitro dimensions demonstrated 
      that standard length tracheostomy tubes are too short for the average critical 
      care patient. We recommend that both the stomal and intratracheal lengths should 
      be made longer by approximately 1 cm and tubes should be redesigned to an angle 
      of 110-120 degrees to allow optimal tracheal placement.
